<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Nasiruddin Nusrat Shah</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">r. <a href="/years/1519">1519</a>–<a href="/years/1533">1533</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/16th-century">16th century</a> · Also known as Nusrat Shah</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Nasiruddin Nusrat Shah was sultan of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bengal">Bengal</a> from 1519 to 1533, the son of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alauddin-husain-shah">Alauddin Husain Shah</a>. He supported the Afghan chiefs of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bihar">Bihar</a> against the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mughal-empire">Mughals</a> and lost to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/babur">Babur</a> at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-ghaghra">Battle of Ghaghra</a> in 1529, then made peace. A patron of Bengali literature, he built the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bara-sona-mosque">Bara Sona Mosque</a> at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gaur">Gaur</a> and the Qadam Rasul shrine. He was assassinated in 1533.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/nasiruddin-nusrat-shah.jpg" alt="Nasiruddin Nusrat Shah" width="320" height="159" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: LouisAragon (uploader) · CC BY-SA 2.5 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:INDIA,_Islamic_Sultanates._Bengal._Nasir_al-Din_Nusrat._AD_1519-1531.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Nasrat was born into an aristocratic Sunni Muslim family. His father <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alauddin-husain-shah">Alauddin Husain Shah</a>, first sultan of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/husain-shahi-dynasty">Hussain Shahi dynasty</a>, had eighteen or twenty-four sons and at least eleven daughters. Nasrat's elder brother Daniyal, the crown prince, was killed in battle against the Assamese, and Nasrat was made crown prince in 1515 and allowed to mint coins of his own. In 1516 he held overall command of an expedition against Arakan, after which <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mrauk-u">Mrauk U</a> again became a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vassal">vassal</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bengal">Bengal</a> and he renamed Chittagong Fatehabad, City of Victory. He married a daughter of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ibrahim-lodi">Ibrahim Lodi</a>, the last sultan of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/delhi">Delhi</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>After his father's death in 1519 Nasrat gave his brothers titles and positions instead of blinding or imprisoning them. He expanded the sultanate, annexing land up to the Tons river after 1522 in alliance with the Lohani rebels, and in 1526 he annexed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mithila">Mithila</a>, killing its ruler Laksminathasimha in battle. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/afghans">Afghans</a> fleeing <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/babur">Babur</a> took refuge in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bengal">Bengal</a>. Babur's envoy in 1527 was imprisoned and later freed, and Nasrat sent gifts. When Nasrat refused to withdraw his troops from the banks of the Ghaghara, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mughal-empire">Mughals</a> defeated the Afghans and Bengalis at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-ghaghra">Battle of Ghaghra</a> on 6 May 1529. Nasrat sued for peace, and Bengal remained independent. He did not join the Afghans at Daurah in 1531.</p><p>A Portuguese mission visited his court in 1521 and described its splendour, and he kept 914 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/war-elephant">war elephants</a>. He ordered the first translation of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mahabharata">Mahabharata</a> into Bengali. He built the Baro Sona Masjid at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gaur">Gaur</a> in 1526, finished the Bagha Mosque in Rajshahi in 1524 and built the Nasrat Gazi Mosque in Barishal in 1532.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Nasrat was assassinated by a slave while visiting his father's grave, and was possibly buried near the Qadam Rasul Mosque at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gaur">Gaur</a>. His son Firuz succeeded him but, three months later, Nasrat's brother Mahmud killed Firuz and took the throne as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ghiyasuddin-mahmud-shah">Ghiyasuddin Mahmud Shah</a>. The reigns of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alauddin-husain-shah">Alauddin Husain Shah</a> and Nasrat Shah are generally regarded as the golden age of the Bengal Sultanate.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Nasiruddin Nasrat Shah” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote><b>Nasiruddin Nusrat Shah</b> (r. 1519–1533) lost ground to Babur, and his successor Ghiyasuddin Mahmud Shah faced the Afghan leader Sher Khan Sur, who defeated his army at Surajgarh in 1534 and took Gaur in 1538.</blockquote><a href="/empire/bengal-sultanate">Bengal Sultanate · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li></ul></section>
